<IndustryPage {...pageData}>
  <p>Charities and nonprofits face a unique software challenge. You need the same capabilities as a commercial business - CRM, accounting, reporting, scheduling - but your workflows, terminology, and compliance requirements are completely different. Donor management is not sales pipeline management. Gift aid is not VAT. Impact reporting is not a profit and loss statement.</p>

  <p>Most off-the-shelf nonprofit software treats charities like small businesses with a twist. You get a commercial CRM with a "nonprofit edition" badge on it, which means you are working around features designed for sales teams while missing the features you actually need - like gift aid calculation, donor relationship tracking across years, volunteer coordination, and impact measurement aligned to your theory of change.</p>

  <p>The result is the familiar charity tech stack: a donor database that does not talk to your accounting software, an event platform that does not sync with your volunteer scheduler, and an impact spreadsheet that someone manually updates once a month. Every system is an island, and the bridges between them are copy-paste and hope.</p>

  <p>Bespoke software solves this by starting with your mission, not a template. Your donor management system knows what gift aid is and handles it natively. Your volunteer platform knows that DBS checks expire and alerts you before they do. Your impact reporting pulls data directly from your activities, not from a spreadsheet someone maintains on their desktop. One system, one source of truth, built around how your charity actually works.</p>

  <p>Compliance is built in from day one. Charity Commission reporting requirements, GDPR consent for donor communications, gift aid record-keeping, and data protection for vulnerable beneficiaries are all designed into the software, not bolted on as an afterthought. For UK charities, data residency matters - your donor data stays in UK-based infrastructure, not in a US data centre governed by a different legal framework.</p>

  <p>A UK-based education charity with 15 staff and 200 volunteers was spending £6,500 a year across four separate SaaS tools - a donor database, an event platform, a volunteer management tool, and accounting software. None of them integrated. Gift aid claims took two days a quarter to process manually. We built a single platform that replaced all four tools: donor management with built-in gift aid automation, event management, volunteer scheduling with DBS tracking, and integration with their existing Xero accounting. Build cost: £22,000. Gift aid processing time dropped from two days to two hours. The charity owns the software outright with no ongoing subscription fees - just hosting costs of around £40/month.</p>

  <p>If your charity is spending more than £3,000 a year on software that does not quite fit, <a href="/services">explore our services</a> or <a href="/contact">get in touch</a> to discuss whether bespoke software makes sense for your mission.</p>
</IndustryPage>